\paragraph{Why SFT over regex?}
SFT retains 97.65\% under marker removal while regex collapses to 28.49\% (\S\ref{sec:nomarker}), and transfers across datasets with negligible loss---confirming diagnostic semantics beyond surface shortcuts.

\paragraph{Design implications.}
Exception events capture 23--27pp of the ${\sim}$25pp headroom over source code, while complete traces recover only 3--6pp---counter to the assumption that more information helps \citep{chen2025tracepile,wang2025dcsh}.
Tier~2 tail truncation is model-dependent (Qwen +10.9pp; CodeLlama +0.9pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:robustness_analysis}).

\paragraph{Boundary conditions: verdict prediction as a proxy task.}
Verdict prediction is a controlled diagnostic setting: binary pass/fail classification with structurally informative exception events.
Our findings may not transfer to tasks where trace content carries non-redundant information---notably fault localization and program repair.
TraceCoder \citep{huang2026tracecoder} demonstrates that selective trace extraction \emph{improves} fault localization accuracy, suggesting that trace utility varies with task complexity: when the downstream task requires localizing the fault within code (not merely detecting its presence), richer trace content provides value that exception-only signals cannot.
The information density effect we quantify is thus specific to tasks where a binary diagnostic signal exists; generalization to multi-class or localization settings remains open.

\paragraph{Data generation considerations.}
Training solutions were sampled from Qwen-2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct; the resulting error distribution (exception types, failure modes) reflects this model's generation biases rather than human programming patterns.
Whether the signal hierarchy generalizes to human-written buggy code with different exception distributions is untested; the GPT-4o frontier comparison (\S\ref{sec:cross_model}) uses zero-shot evaluation on the same benchmark, not independent data.

